<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
This file defines the public `plugin_odometry_publisher` wrapper.
The goal of this wrapper is to emit the XML for a Gazebo Sim `OdometryPublisher` plugin attached to
a model.

This wrapper publishes one `gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher` `<plugin>`
block.

What this plugin publishes:
- A `gz::msgs::Odometry` message on `odom_topic`.
- A `gz::msgs::OdometryWithCovariance` message on `odom_covariance_topic`.
- A transform from `odom_frame` to `robot_base_frame` on `tf_topic`.

How to interpret that transform:
- Gazebo uses the xml tags `odom_frame` and `robot_base_frame`.
- The published pose and transform always describe
  `T:${odom_frame} -> ${robot_base_frame}`.
- This means the plugin reports the pose of the robot base with respect to
  the frame named in `odom_frame`.

Why the name can be confusing:
- The system is called `OdometryPublisher`, but it does not force
  `odom_frame` to be the robot odometry frame in the ROS navigation sense.
- You are free to choose `odom_frame`.
- If you set `odom_frame` to a world-fixed frame such as `map`, `world`, or
  `sim_world`, then the published pose is better understood as a
  simulator-provided ground-truth localization signal.
- If you set `odom_frame` to the robot odometry frame, then the same plugin
  output can also play the role of odometry.

How offsets are applied:
- `xyz_offset` and `rpy_offset` shift the reported robot pose relative to the
  robot body frame before publishing.
- `xyz_global_offset` and `rpy_global_offset` shift the published fixed frame
  relative to Gazebo's world frame.
- When all offsets are zero, the published pose is expressed directly with
  respect to Gazebo's world frame.

Practical guidance:
- Use a world-fixed name in `odom_frame` only if you really want this plugin
  to publish global pose with respect to that frame.
- Be careful when also using another system that publishes
  `T:odom -> base`, such as a wheel-odometry pipeline.
- Be especially careful if another node, for example `fake_localization`,
  also applies offsets or publishes related transforms.
- Use `/tf` as `tf_topic` only if you intentionally want this transform to be
  inserted directly into the main TF stream.

The caller has to decide:
- The fixed odometry frame name through `odom_frame`.
- The robot base frame name through `robot_base_frame`.
- The publication frequency through `odom_publish_frequency`.
- The odometry topics through `odom_topic` and `odom_covariance_topic`.
- The TF topic through `tf_topic`.
- Whether odometry is reported in 2D or 3D through `dimensions`.
- The local and global pose offsets through the `*_offset` parameters.
- The Gaussian noise value through `gaussian_noise`.

This wrapper does not resolve namespaces or choose frame names. Those
responsibilities belong in a higher-level robot macro.

To find the reference for the system Gazebo plugins:
1. Go to: https://gazebosim.org/libs/
2. Click in the link 'API & Tutorials' for the 'Sim' section.
3. Click in the link 'gz::sim::systems'

At the time of writing this text (March 2026), the link 'gz::sim::systems' points to the
URL 'https://gazebosim.org/api/sim/10/namespacegz_1_1sim_1_1systems.html', for API reference 10.1.1

https://gazebosim.org/api/sim/10/classgz_1_1sim_1_1systems_1_1OdometryPublisher.html
-->
<robot name="plugin_odometry_publisher_macro" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro"
       xmlns:gz="http://gazebosim.org/schema">
  <xacro:macro name="plugin_odometry_publisher"
               params="odom_frame
                       robot_base_frame
                       odom_publish_frequency:=50
                       odom_topic:=''
                       odom_covariance_topic:=''
                       tf_topic:=''
                       dimensions:=2
                       xyz_offset:='0 0 0'
                       rpy_offset:='0 0 0'
                       xyz_global_offset:='0 0 0'
                       rpy_global_offset:='0 0 0'
                       gaussian_noise:=0">
    <xacro:if value="${dimensions not in (2, 3)}">
      ${xacro.fatal("plugin_odometry_publisher: dimensions must be 2 or 3")}
    </xacro:if>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher">
      <!--
      The published odometry and transform always describe
      `T:odom_frame -> robot_base_frame`.
      -->
      <odom_frame>${odom_frame}</odom_frame>
      <robot_base_frame>${robot_base_frame}</robot_base_frame>

      <!--
      Gazebo already knows the pose (position and orientation) of the robot in the simulation world:

      rawPose = pose_robot_in_gazebo_world (also known as T: gazebo_world -> robot_base_frame)

      In this comment, `T: reference_frame -> referenced_frame` means:
      The position and orientation of `referenced_frame` expressed in `reference_frame`.

      pose_in_reference_frame =
        T: reference_frame -> referenced_frame * pose_in_referenced_frame

      With that convention, publishing the robot pose with respect to a
      different fixed frame, for example `map`, requires this transform:

      pose_robot_in_map =
        T: map -> gazebo_world * pose_robot_in_gazebo_world

      The `xyz_global_offset` and `rpy_global_offset` tags define the pose (position and
      orientation)  of `odom_frame` expressed in Gazebo's simulation world:

      globalOffset = T: gazebo_world -> odom_frame

      Therefore, the plugin must invert `globalOffset` before applying it:

      globalOffset.Inverse() = T: odom_frame -> gazebo_world

      The plugin then does this:

      pose = globalOffset.Inverse() * rawPose

      Conceptually, this produces:

      pose = T: odom_frame -> gazebo_world * T: gazebo_world -> robot_base_frame
           = T: odom_frame -> robot_base_frame

      If `odom_frame` is `map`, the published odometry pose is therefore the
      robot pose expressed in `map`, not directly in Gazebo's simulation world.

      Note: these global offset tags are available from Gazebo Sim 10.
      Older Gazebo Sim versions will ignore those fields.
      -->
      <xyz_global_offset>${xyz_global_offset}</xyz_global_offset>
      <rpy_global_offset>${rpy_global_offset}</rpy_global_offset>

      <!--
      The local offset changes which rigid point of the robot is reported.

      Gazebo starts from the pose of `robot_base_frame` (position and orientation of the robot
      frame) in the simulation world:

      rawPose = T: gazebo_world -> robot_base_frame

      The local offset defines a pose (position and orientation) expressed with respect to
      `robot_base_frame`. That pose could represent another frame rigidly attached to the robot,
      for example the frame of a lidar:

      offset = T: robot_base_frame -> offset_frame

      The global offset changes the frame in which the result is expressed.
      The local offset changes the robot point whose pose is reported.
      Together, both offsets describe the pose (position and orientation) of that robot point in the
      selected fixed frame.
      The resulting transform starts in `odom_frame`, passes through `robot_base_frame`, and ends at
      the local `offset_frame`:

      pose = T: odom_frame -> gazebo_world * rawPose * offset
           = T: odom_frame -> gazebo_world * T: gazebo_world -> robot_base_frame * T: robot_base_frame -> offset_frame
           = T: odom_frame -> gazebo_world * T: gazebo_world -> offset_frame
           = T: odom_frame -> offset_frame

      For example, if the offset describes the pose of a lidar with respect to `robot_base_frame`,
      the plugin publishes the pose of that lidar in the fixed frame named by `odom_frame`.
      -->
      <xyz_offset>${xyz_offset}</xyz_offset>
      <rpy_offset>${rpy_offset}</rpy_offset>

      <!--
      Gazebo uses `gaussian_noise` to populate the covariance information of
      the published odometry-with-covariance message.
      -->
      <gaussian_noise>${gaussian_noise}</gaussian_noise>
      <odom_publish_frequency>${odom_publish_frequency}</odom_publish_frequency>

      <xacro:if value="${odom_topic != ''}">
        <odom_topic>${odom_topic}</odom_topic>
      </xacro:if>

      <xacro:if value="${odom_covariance_topic != ''}">
        <odom_covariance_topic>${odom_covariance_topic}</odom_covariance_topic>
      </xacro:if>

      <xacro:if value="${tf_topic != ''}">
        <tf_topic>${tf_topic}</tf_topic>
      </xacro:if>

      <dimensions>${dimensions}</dimensions>
    </plugin>
  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
